iterations.reply:
I left out both operations requests. This node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/response-channel`, and the body imports nothing else. A file write and an outbound HTTP fetch would each need a capability the node doesn't have. The HTTP fetch would also send the user's message text to an external service, which the node's contract doesn't allow. Logging and enrichment need their own node with a grant that covers them.

The node sends the reply and checks the confirmation. If delivery fails or the session id is empty, it panics. With `panic = "abort"` that traps, so the node never returns a result that breaks `ensures`.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        let confirmation = response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        // ensures: delivered == true and present(session).
        // If the host did not confirm delivery, trap rather than return a
        // confirmation that violates the contract.
        if !confirmation.delivered || confirmation.session.is_empty() {
            panic!("SendReply: channel did not confirm delivery with a session id");
        }

        confirmation
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

